1
00:00:01,333 --> 00:00:03,366
You're staring at four things in your cart

2
00:00:03,566 --> 00:00:05,300
None of them seems outrageous

3
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:09,000
But when you hit checkout your balance only covers two

4
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:12,300
In that moment what you delete isn't just two items

5
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,966
It's two other lives you could have lived

6
00:00:15,166 --> 00:00:16,700
This is scarcity

7
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:19,266
It's not something only broke people run into

8
00:00:19,266 --> 00:00:24,000
As long as you want more than you can give scarcity is already happening

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:28,500
When most people hear scarcity they think of being poor

10
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:31,733
As if only an empty balance earns that word

11
00:00:31,833 --> 00:00:32,800
Not really

12
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:35,333
Scarcity isn't about whether you have anything

13
00:00:35,366 --> 00:00:38,200
It says your resources will always be limited

14
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:41,533
While the things you want to do always line up ahead of them

15
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:43,300
Money is limited

16
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:44,966
Time is limited

17
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:47,200
Attention is limited too

18
00:00:47,366 --> 00:00:49,133
You can push your salary up a bit

19
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:51,366
Pack your schedule a little tighter

20
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:58,400
But you can't spend the same evening having dinner with family hitting the gym taking a course and doing perfect work

21
00:00:58,566 --> 00:00:59,933
That's not a ability problem

22
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,866
That's scarcity at work

23
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:03,966
This is where economics begins

24
00:01:04,066 --> 00:01:07,133
Because once resources are limited you must choose

25
00:01:07,366 --> 00:01:09,866
Choose this and you set that aside for now

26
00:01:09,866 --> 00:01:11,700
Not because the other one isn't worth it

27
00:01:11,766 --> 00:01:15,266
But because the same resource can't be spent twice in the same moment

28
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:17,400
That sounds like common sense

29
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,766
Yet our most common mistake is pretending it doesn't exist

30
00:01:20,866 --> 00:01:23,000
We keep adding to the cart

31
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:24,900
Keep filling the weekends

32
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:26,933
Keep saying yes to more promises

33
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,900
Then at the moment of checkout we blame ourselves for never being enough

34
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:33,566
Scarcity isn't life punishing you

35
00:01:33,766 --> 00:01:35,933
It's simply the starting point of every choice

36
00:01:36,066 --> 00:01:37,466
You can't have it all

37
00:01:37,466 --> 00:01:40,466
So you'd better know what you want to keep most

38
00:01:41,566 --> 00:01:47,500
Last month Xiao Zhou paid her salary minus rent and fixed bills with three thousand yuan left

39
00:01:47,766 --> 00:01:50,700
She sat on her bed deciding where that money should go

40
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,533
A toothache the filling costs twelve hundred

41
00:01:53,666 --> 00:01:57,100
Her mom's birthday is coming she wants an eight hundred yuan coat

42
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:00,100
A course she's wanted forever six hundred

43
00:02:00,466 --> 00:02:03,366
Friends invited her on a weekend trip about a thousand

44
00:02:03,633 --> 00:02:05,133
She added up the numbers

45
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,200
Three thousand six

46
00:02:06,633 --> 00:02:08,500
Not a single one is wasted money

47
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:10,366
The tooth can't wait forever

48
00:02:10,566 --> 00:02:12,666
She doesn't want to shortchange her mom's birthday

49
00:02:12,766 --> 00:02:14,733
She's had that course bookmarked for six months

50
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:18,500
The trip is the only breather she wanted to give herself after so long

51
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:20,133
But she only has three thousand

52
00:02:20,466 --> 00:02:22,766
Xiao Zhou stared at that list frustrated

53
00:02:23,133 --> 00:02:26,200
She felt she couldn't even sort out such a small thing

54
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,133
In the end she crossed out the trip

55
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:29,466
Fix the tooth first

56
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:30,866
Buy the coat

57
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,333
The course can wait

58
00:02:32,466 --> 00:02:34,366
This decision wasn't brilliant

59
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:37,600
It's just that three thousand yuan had already drawn her a line

60
00:02:37,833 --> 00:02:40,533
Inside the line is what she can hold this time

61
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:44,300
Outside the line is what she also wants but can't carry yet

62
00:02:44,466 --> 00:02:46,366
That line is scarcity

63
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:49,233
It doesn't judge whether she's a good daughter

64
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:51,300
Or whether she's ambitious enough

65
00:02:51,466 --> 00:02:56,000
It just forces several equally important things to queue up inside limited resources

66
00:02:56,466 --> 00:02:58,600
Later Xiao Zhou got a bonus

67
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:01,200
She thought this time it would finally be enough

68
00:03:01,366 --> 00:03:03,566
But new choices came along with it

69
00:03:03,866 --> 00:03:05,666
She wants a more comfortable chair

70
00:03:05,766 --> 00:03:07,533
Wants to travel a little farther

71
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,766
Wants to keep some money as a cushion

72
00:03:10,066 --> 00:03:11,133
A bit more money

73
00:03:11,133 --> 00:03:13,000
A few more wants

74
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:14,300
That's not greed

75
00:03:14,666 --> 00:03:17,766
It's that scarcity never lives only inside three thousand yuan

76
00:03:17,866 --> 00:03:23,733
It lives in every moment resources are limited while life reaches out with many hands at once

77
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:28,166
Understanding scarcity isn't about buying nothing from now on

78
00:03:28,433 --> 00:03:31,700
Nor about feeling guilty toward every wish

79
00:03:31,966 --> 00:03:35,066
It just reminds you not to treat choosing as failing

80
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:38,300
Crossing out the trip doesn't mean you don't deserve rest

81
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:41,466
Delaying the course doesn't mean you don't want to grow

82
00:03:41,666 --> 00:03:45,766
You're simply admitting that today's resources can only take care of some things first

83
00:03:46,133 --> 00:03:47,733
That admission matters

84
00:03:47,933 --> 00:03:53,266
Because people who deny scarcity keep mistaking every trade-off for proof they're not good enough

85
00:03:53,533 --> 00:03:55,133
So they overdraw money

86
00:03:55,133 --> 00:03:56,466
Overdraw time

87
00:03:56,633 --> 00:03:58,566
And overdraw their patience with themselves

88
00:03:58,866 --> 00:04:01,200
Next time a pile of things you all want shows up

89
00:04:01,266 --> 00:04:04,333
Don't ask first why can't I have everything

90
00:04:04,733 --> 00:04:08,600
Ask first what is most worth putting my resources into right now

91
00:04:08,933 --> 00:04:10,133
That's not scheming

92
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:12,133
It's putting your life in order

93
00:04:12,266 --> 00:04:14,466
Scarcity gives choices their weight

94
00:04:14,533 --> 00:04:17,600
And finally lets you see what you truly value

95
00:04:17,766 --> 00:04:21,066
The two items you deleted from the cart aren't necessarily a loss

96
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:25,366
Sometimes you're making room for the two you most wanted to keep

97
00:04:25,633 --> 00:04:27,700
Next time we'll talk about opportunity cost

98
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,300
The thing you set down was it worth it
